{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196eKhGck6KWZpsoJbsi4TF","type":"tool_result","content":"  needs it (Section~\\ref{sec:true}).\n\\item One reference of the table, \\citet{LY2002ref5}, is not held. Its row,\n  interval thickness, is proved from two other\n  references of the table\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:true}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{Open problems.} Two values of the MOSP corpus,\n\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-2\\_0} and \\texttt{Random-125-125-2-3\\_0}, have never\nbeen certified. As of 6 October 2026 the split search is still refuting the\nvalue minus one on each and has found no order of the smaller value\n(Section~\\ref{sec:practice}). Of the 17,714 classes of the dataset, 1,627 have\nno certified pathwidth. One more budget step for every open class within the\nsize our implementation handles costs at most 500 core-hours, but on the Rome graphs each such step has closed only about\n40\\% of what it attempted, so the graph sets will not close by budget alone.\nThe 610 MOSP instances with 400 to 1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above\n1,024 vertices per component, the limit of our implementation, are beyond an\nexact search. Beyond budgets, a class is certified without search\nonly when a lower bound meets its witness (Section~\\ref{sec:dataset}). The\nopen classes are therefore also a test set for stronger lower bounds on\npathwidth.\n21:\\subsection{Experimental setup}\n22-\\label{sec:setup}\n23-\n24-\\paragraph{Machine and code.} Every run in this section used one\n25-machine, an AMD Ryzen~9 9950X (16 cores, 32 hardware threads) with 128\\,GB of\n26-memory under Ubuntu Linux. Both searches, the open-stacks customer search and\n27-its port to graphs, are written in C (gcc 15.2, \\texttt{-O3\n28--march=native}) and driven from Python~3.12. A Python implementation of the\n29-same rules serves as a reference for cross-checks and emits the certificates\n30-of Section~\\ref{sec:certs}. The repaired tests are the default in every\n31-implementation; the published tests remain available as an option.\n32-\n33-\\paragraph{Runs.} The basic job asks one question: is the optimum of this\n34-instance at most $k$? A \\emph{worker} is one single-threaded process that\n35-answers such questions, one at a time. A \\emph{node} is one state visited by\n36-the search, the unit in which we report effort. Up to 32 workers ran at\n37-once on the machine, so wall-clock times carry load noise. We therefore\n38-compare node counts and report time only in core-hours. A \\emph{pair} runs\n39-the same question twice in the same worker, once under the published rules\n40-and once under the repaired rules, alternating which goes first. A\n41-\\emph{descent} solves a whole graph: it lowers $k$ from an upper bound until a\n42-refutation proves the last value optimal. Each table states its time or node\n43-budget.\n44-\n45-\\paragraph{Configurations.} The open-stacks runs use two configurations of the\n46-rules of Section~\\ref{sec:search}, which differ only in the better move. The\n47-\\emph{base} configuration leaves it off. The configuration \\emph{with better\n48-move} switches it on for instances whose customers need at most five patterns on\n49-average. This is the setting under which the customer search certified the\n50-benchmark values it proved.\n51-\n52-\\paragraph{Instances.} The \\emph{MOSP corpus} is the 6,376 instance files of\n53-four public open-stacks collections, one of which is distributed twice. They\n54-are the 2005 Constraint Modelling Challenge \\citep{SmithGent2005}, with a\n55-second copy of 46 of its files under other names, the random\n56-instances of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} (30 to 125 customers), those of\n57-\\citet{FaggioliBentivoglio1998}, and the 24 industrial SCOOP instances. They\n58-are the first rows of Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}. A Chu--Stuckey name such as\n59-\\texttt{Random-125-125-2-4\\_0} gives the number of customers, the number of\n60-patterns, the generator's density class and the instance number. The graph\n61-benchmarks are those of Section~\\ref{sec:names} and Table~\\ref{tab:dataset}.\n../dataset.md:26:24,643,531 vertices. The dataset's records cover graphs of 0 to 1,024\n../dataset.md:27:vertices with a witnessed value; everything above the C engine's 1,024\n../dataset.md:318:*open, engine*: classes without a certified value whose largest component fits the C engine (≤ 1024 vertices); *open, beyond*: larger, or read by header only. *spent 10-02*: the repaired-rules runs of 2026-10-02 (`solver_fix.md` item 08); *spent here*: this item's run (witnesses and first runs).\n../dataset.md:373:The in-flight values are `certified:refutation` in `solutions/` (certified before the repair) and carry a note in the dataset; the two open ones are `solution`. *Item 07 price*: `solver_fix.md` item 07, which says those prices are low. The one density-2 125 × 125 re-certification completed so far, `Random-125-125-2-4_0`, took 154.1 core-hours (split run, refuted 2026-10-03 02:55) against an item 07 price of 47.5.\n../dataset.md:409:  took 154 core-hours against an item 07 price of 47.5. The two open\n../dataset.md:420:- **Frinhani et al. (400–1000) and everything above 1,024 vertices** are\n../dataset.md:421:  out of reach of an exact search: 610 + 154 classes. The C engine stops at\n../dataset.md:422:  1,024 vertices per component, and the model's prediction at 400 (10²⁹\n../solver_fix.md:135:| pinned (both `DEFINITE_CEX`, Bug A, Bug B, `RUN_LOST_CEX`), every `k`, `L` 0–1 | 5 | 26,690 | 23,312 | 109,496 | 1,317 | 974 | 6 | 1,024 |\n../solver_fix.md:262:36,274,154). That figure is only a hint, from capped runs on small and","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a41b07d6-4bc0-41d6-bf63-23515c47cd6e","uuid":"3a017bb9-f487-48bd-b7c1-67fa0deae02e","timestamp":"2026-10-06T21:06:13.427Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  needs it (Section~\\ref{sec:true}).\n\\item One reference of the table, \\citet{LY2002ref5}, is not held. I